{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of DX-A2 at head e38f9cc4b7780bfb9a80de435e8b43b6036109c3, tree 8d630287, for request 1c124ebf and review request fefd1cb9. Verdict: APPROVED, with one small departure from ruling 366b3989 named below for the planner.\n\nThe head adds two commits to the reviewed head 7424aafc. I read the note first, then the diff, against the two items of review 18f90534, and I rest on reviews 0924ce2e and 18f90534 for the 55-path implementation. That earlier reading covered the closed wire, all five transports, authority at each wake, registration and deadline races, the causal-chain refresh, the wait's separation from the projection flight, the below-floor rule, `followReceipt` and the card refresh.\n\n## Items of 18f90534\n\n1. A causal reader below the floor. Resolved.\n   - `Reads.minimum` now answers `archived` for an entry position below the floor, right after `#open` authenticates the request and before any limit check. That matches `Reads.entry`, so the barrier is no longer stricter than the read.\n   - A native case signs a fresh entry request after the intent window has passed and the row is gone. `entry`, `followReceipt` and `entry` with a minimum all give the same archived answer.\n   - A stranger with a future minimum gets only that same pointer, with no wait and no `behind.current`. A retained row stays `forbidden` for that stranger.\n2. The hosted enrollment 409. Resolved.\n   - `hosted.ts:550` sends a qualified sequence minimum, so a wrong retained hash and a changed signed input both reach the 409 at line 553.\n   - `hosted-enrol-readback.test.ts` runs both faults and the passing case against the actual `HostedAgent`, SQLite custody, signing and HTTP decoders, with a labelled in-memory peer. It also checks that the bearer is issued only in the passing case and that the retained envelope stays the original.\n\n## One departure, for the planner\n\nRuling 366b3989 says that below the floor `entry` answers `archived` \"after authority and the ScopeRef and incarnation checks\". `Reads.minimum` now returns `archived` from inside the position check, and `ReadWait.until` returns any refusal before it compares the kind and incarnation (`read-wait.ts`). So `entry(seq, {at: \u003canother incarnation\u003e, head})` at a position below the floor answers `archived`, not `wrong-incarnation`. `docs/scopes.md:774` says that another incarnation returns `wrong-incarnation`, and no test covers this case.\n\nI don't block on it. It grants no access, the pointer is what a plain read of that position returns, and `followReceipt` on main gives the same answer. The planner can accept it with one sentence in `docs/scopes.md`, or ask for the comparison to move first, which needs a source change and a full gate.\n\n## The gates\n\n- All earlier gate failures stay failed, and the fourth repair gate stays a pass for its older source. Gate 5 at ba895314 exits 1 under the outer sandbox restriction the note describes, and it keeps its row.\n- Gate 6 at ba895314, tree 0170d16b, passes all five stages. Its receipt exits 0 with complete capture, the same head and tree before and after, and no dropped bytes. Plain passes 3,159 with one skip and Native 866 with two skips.\n- From ba895314 to the head, only the note and the manifest change.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 5068dab4 gives 72619aac, with no conflict.\n- The note is the commitment's report, filed last, and the request rests on 55 artifacts at this head.\n- I ran the owning files once each, with no whole suite: `hosted-enrol-readback` and client `read-minimum`, 10 of 10; native `read-minimum`, `client` and `application-projection-native`, 10 of 10; `security-entry-points.test.mjs`, 10 of 10. The builder worktree stayed clean.\n- `node scripts/check-kernel-boundary.mjs` checks 127 production sources with no refusals.\n- The evidence verifies. The directory has 1,836 files and 384,281,554 bytes, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean, and no file is unlisted or writable. The external full manifest has SHA-256 5e3a08e1, and all 1,836 of its rows match. All 110 files cited in the committed manifest match.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 761 links, 0 broken. check-notes from 5dc9765f: 0 warnings. check-commit-messages: 12 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\nPoints 1 to 6 of 18f90534 stand. In short: a late seal after expiry answers `unavailable`, an unscoped causal reader gets no wait for a future entry, the two projection preparations run outside the flight's timer, a throw in the projection's barrier can reach HTTP 500, chain updates aren't shared between waiters, and no test covers a floor advancing during a wait. The waiter cap stays deferred to the planner's observability design.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe barrier stays in Scope, the handle and the hosted route own their permanent refusals, and the read stays one contract across transports. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nAuthority holds at every wake. The below-floor answer grants nothing beyond a plain read, and a stranger gains no current-head oracle. Permanent mismatches read as permanent again. Approved, with the departure above disclosed.\n\n## Simplification\n\nBoth final repairs are a few lines, and they remove the barrier's disagreement with the read. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the two items hold, gate 6 passes with a documents-only tail, and the evidence verifies. Dispositions: 0924ce2e and 18f90534 resolved, with the one departure above.\n\nNo hosted or deployment claim follows from this review. The landing check will follow the landing. The head changes Worker code, so the planner decides on the deploy. Request 1c124ebf's order, and the documentation rewrite's edits to `docs/scopes.md` and `docs/mcp.md`, mean that whichever lands second merges by hand."